        "id": "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be",
        "title": "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "a7ad19ee-c937-5a75-b1b6-4803c2c7bf3b",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(- 8 x)\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{C_1}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r+\\int_{C_2}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path $C_1$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$r_1(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j,$$\n\nwith $t$ running from $0$ to $2$. Along this path, $x=t$ and $y=t^{2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$F(r_1(t))=(- 8 t)\\,i+(- 8 t^{2})\\,j.$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$r_1'(t)=(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$F(r_1(t))\\cdot r_1'(t)=(- 8 t)(1)+(- 8 t^{2})(2 t)=- 16 t^{3} - 8 t.$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\\int_{C_1}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{0}^{2}(- 16 t^{3} - 8 t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\left[- 4 t^{4} - 4 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}=-80.$$\n\n$$\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=(-80)+(16)=-64.$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
